WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.015 --> 00:00:06.569
If you don't know, Journey to the Microcosmos
has its own Microcosmos Microscope.

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:12.442
A microscope we designed specifically for
people who are new to the hobby of Microscopy.

00:00:12.442 --> 00:00:16.457
Who maybe want to discover their 
very first tardigrade.

00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:21.624
It's a really wonderful moment, I had mine
about two and a half years ago.

00:00:21.624 --> 00:00:26.295
And with the Microcosmos Microscope you
are able to attach your phone to it

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:30.642
So you can take pictures and video 
of the things that you discover.

00:00:30.642 --> 00:00:34.907
When I first wanted to try out microscopy,
I found the step of finding the right

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:38.935
microscope to start with very intimidating,
so we're just trying to make that easier

00:00:38.935 --> 00:00:39.978
for everybody.

00:00:39.978 --> 00:00:45.062
You can find it and a bunch of other stuff like 
great shirts and socks to celebrate

00:00:45.062 --> 00:00:48.703
the microscopic world around us at
microcosmos.store. 

00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:14.980
If you need to nominate an ambassador 
to represent the microcosmos,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.891
you have to go with the tardigrade.

00:01:17.891 --> 00:01:21.440
They are weird, adorable, and very hardy,–

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.192
a combination of traits that has made them
many people’s first entry point into the

00:01:26.192 --> 00:01:27.788
microscopic world.

00:01:27.788 --> 00:01:33.497
And no matter how much time you spend with them,
tardigrades will find new ways to surprise you.

00:01:33.497 --> 00:01:38.110
In fact, when we’ve looked back on our journey
through the microcosmos and our encounters

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.920
with tardigrades, we’ve noticed that many
of those strange sightings involve something

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.420
that doesn’t immediately come to mind with
tardigrades:

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.220
sex.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.890
So today, we want to thread together these
tales of tardigrade mating, and not just because

00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:00.689
it’s fun, but also because there’s a lot
to learn from understanding how tardigrades

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.566
make more of themselves.

00:02:02.566 --> 00:02:07.329
Let’s start with one of our early videos
about tardigrades, where we were introduced

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:14.030
a pregnant water bear whose eggs were beginning
to develop even as her body remained still,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.957
and whose fate we’ll get to see play out
later in this compilation.

00:02:26.522 --> 00:02:32.420
We don’t know about you, but here on the
Microcosmos team, we find it hard not to smile

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:39.909
as we watch tardigrades with their cute, pitch-black
eyes and little feet ambling across the smooth

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:43.075
glass surface of the microscope slide.

00:02:43.075 --> 00:02:48.413
Moss piglets, water bears—whatever you call
them, they’re fascinating to watch, and

00:02:48.413 --> 00:02:52.768
even more fascinating to try and understand.

00:02:52.768 --> 00:02:57.069
They are a puzzle, and we humans are the puzzler.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:06.356
And so we figured, sure, we’ve already focused
on tardigrades in a previous episode.

00:03:06.356 --> 00:03:09.620
But we cannot possibly leave it there.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:16.486
After all, like the microscopic world around
them, tardigrades always have more to show us.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.890
Our water bears are collected from ponds and
growing moss, environments that give them

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.440
the water and food they need to thrive.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.450
And so, behind the scenes, we like to give
them a bit of attention, making sure that

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:38.330
they are well fed and comfortable in their
slides.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:45.030
After all, a drop of water is barely anything
to us, but to a tardigrade, a thing so tiny

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:51.048
and so good at surviving, that drop can be
everything.

00:03:51.048 --> 00:03:57.724
Tardigrades of course live their lives, absent
of any kind of intention to teach us anything.

00:03:57.724 --> 00:04:00.000
They don’t even know we’re here.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.069
But watching is a form of interaction, and
one that reveals the interface between the

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:11.879
micro, the macro, and even the cosmic.

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:16.989
So let’s start with a part of the tardigrade
body whose weirdness we haven’t had the

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:22.180
chance to fully appreciate here yet: their
mouths.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:29.050
The opening of the tardigrade mouth is a distinct
ring, giving way to a tube that connects to

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:36.396
the pharyngeal bulb, which helps the tardigrade
suction out the juicy contents of its meal.

00:04:36.396 --> 00:04:44.169
But to get to the juice, the tardigrade first
needs to pierce its target, relying on a pair

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:50.860
of stylets that poke out of the mouth ring
when it comes time to feed.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.917
When the tardigrade molts, they shed their
stylets and replace them with a new pair.

00:04:56.917 --> 00:05:03.110
Tardigrades are omnivorous, they’re open
to eating bacteria, plants, and animals.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.543
For some species, their meal might even be
another tardigrade.

00:05:07.543 --> 00:05:13.000
But for the species here, our samples contain
plenty of algae and plant matter for them

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.639
to dine on, collected from the same watery
and earthy worlds as the tardigrades these

00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:24.840
bits of organic matter keep them well fed
like a built-in buffet.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:31.020
But there are other times where bearing witness
to the microcosmos takes a little more adaptation

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.751
on our part.

00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:40.660
Recently, we found a water bear with its half-shed
cuticle filled with eggs, still attached to

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:46.125
its body in what amounts to parenting in the
microcosmos.

00:05:46.125 --> 00:05:52.555
As you might imagine, we were more than a
little curious to watch these eggs develop.

00:05:52.555 --> 00:05:58.729
But if you keep a slide on a microscope for
too long, the water will evaporate, taking

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:05.634
with it the moisture the tardigrade needs
to exist outside of its dessicated, dormant state.

00:06:05.634 --> 00:06:11.500
So we kept these slides in humidity chambers
and took extra care to make sure the coverslip

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.981
wouldn’t crush the developing tardigrades.

00:06:14.981 --> 00:06:17.876
And then…we watched.

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:26.569
The tardigrade though was still.

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:33.319
This video is from our sixth day of observation,
the only sign of life being the absence of

00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:34.835
decomposition.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:45.199
Three days later, we can see the eggs are
developing, and there are even little tardigrade

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:47.080
heads appearing.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:54.590
The mother still seems the same, attached
to her eggs and seemingly still alive despite

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:58.340
her complete stillness.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:04.580
When it comes time to hatch, the embryos
will use their stylets and pharyngeal pump

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:10.229
to make their way out, sucking in water to
increase the pressure on the egg from the

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:13.729
inside until the shell breaks.

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:19.250
But…we’re still waiting for that day to
come with this tardigrade brood.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:26.949
Embryonic development in tardigrade can last
from 5 days to more than 100, varying by species

00:07:26.949 --> 00:07:30.795
and dependent on environmental factors like
temperature.

00:07:30.795 --> 00:07:36.310
The thing with biology, no matter what scale
of creature you’re looking at, is that you’re

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:40.104
always on their schedule, not the other way
around.

00:07:46.917 --> 00:07:53.449
Food and birth probably seem a bit domestic
compared to some of the more recent tardigradian

00:07:53.449 --> 00:07:55.751
exploits you may have heard about.

00:07:55.751 --> 00:08:02.460
Earlier in 2019, Israel sent a spacecraft
called Beresheet to the moon, taking with

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:08.650
it, among many things, a digital archive of
almost the entirety of English Wikipedia and

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:18.000
also thousands of tardigrades, dried out in
their ball-like tun form and ready for lift off.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.960
Seconds before Beresheet was supposed to land,
however, it lost contact with Mission control

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:32.500
and crashed instead, leaving one very obvious
question: what will happen to those thousands

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:38.110
of tardigrades, potentially spilled across
the surface of the moon.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:43.741
Our seeming penchant for sending tardigrades
into space has taught us that they’re able

00:08:43.741 --> 00:08:46.292
to tolerate vacuums.

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:50.918
Without liquid water, of course, the tardigrades
will remain in their dormant state.

00:08:50.918 --> 00:08:57.080
Maybe if someone is able to return to that
crash site decades from now, they might manage

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:02.480
to find tardigrades that survived that crash,
and there’s a chance that with a little

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.519
water, they could come back to life.

00:09:08.411 --> 00:09:13.430
We’re still working to understand all of
the tricks of tardigrade survival, how does

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:19.000
it play out in their DNA and proteins, and
why some tardigrade species seem to be better

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.706
than others.

00:09:20.706 --> 00:09:26.151
This is part of the fun of tardigrades and
our collective fascination with them.

00:09:26.151 --> 00:09:32.120
We’re all learning together here on planet
earth, like a giant science classroom, asking

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:38.820
important questions like, “Is that round
thing the mouth?” and “What does happen

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.317
when you put a tardigrade on the moon?”

00:09:41.317 --> 00:09:49.019
Maybe this is our 21st century remix of Apollo,
a conversation between microcosmos and good

00:09:49.019 --> 00:09:51.551
old-fashioned cosmos.

00:09:51.551 --> 00:09:58.560
Science is ultimately a mix of deliberate
and accidental questions, taking on many forms

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.346
to suit our available tools.

00:10:01.346 --> 00:10:07.052
Sometimes science is a detailed study, and
sometimes it’s a crash landing.

00:10:07.052 --> 00:10:12.810
It can be a collector of microbes and a master
of microscopes, carefully tending to samples

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.070
to meet the needs of their invisible residents.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:22.930
And sometimes it’s you, watching along,
making your own observations, maybe even thinking

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:27.278
of new questions that no one has ever thought
to ask before.

00:10:32.723 --> 00:10:36.277
In our next video, we’re jumping straight
to the point.

00:10:36.277 --> 00:10:42.110
This is a video about tardigrade sex, featuring,
you know, tardigrades having sex.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:49.091
And the process is about as perplexing to watch 
as you might imagine tardigrade mating would be. 

00:10:51.665 --> 00:10:58.370
Welcome to season two of Journey to the Microcosmos,
and we are starting things off with a pun

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:01.209
very much intended--a bang.

00:11:01.209 --> 00:11:04.779
Or more accurately, a multitude of bangs.

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:08.110
And we should warn you, it gets weird: there's
poop!

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.385
There's molted skin!

00:11:09.385 --> 00:11:15.176
There's the inexhaustible spirit of the tardigrade
in flagrante!

00:11:19.551 --> 00:11:22.041
So, how did we get here?

00:11:22.041 --> 00:11:26.959
Well, sex, obviously, if we're talking in
the grand biological scheme of things.

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:33.105
It is for some, an evolutionarily advantageous
way of making offspring.

00:11:33.105 --> 00:11:38.100
But we're talking about tardigrade sex right
now in part because of a pregnant water bear

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.637
that we started tracking in an episode last
season.

00:11:41.637 --> 00:11:47.190
Even though the mother-to-be was completely
motionless, we were able to see the heads

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:55.130
of baby tardigrades forming in the eggs, and
we were excited to record and share the moment

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:58.430
of all those moss piglets hatching.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:05.951
Unfortunately, nature had different plans,
and we'll talk about that more in our next episode.

00:12:05.951 --> 00:12:12.130
But James, our master of microbes, has been
hard at work documenting the lives of the

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:13.798
tardigrades in his care.

00:12:13.798 --> 00:12:20.329
And that means, not only have we been able
to find more tardigrades hatching, we've seen

00:12:20.329 --> 00:12:27.180
some particularly vivid demonstrations of
the steps that came before.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:33.850
These hardy animals can reproduce asexually
via parthenogenesis, but our recent samples

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:41.269
have been full of frisky water bears, and
that is what we are going to focus on today.

00:12:41.269 --> 00:12:47.310
Sure, tardigrades contain an immense capacity
for survival that astounds and boggles the

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:53.740
mind, and it is a joy and a privilege to share
the biology and visual wonders of their lives

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:58.130
alongside all of the other tiny organisms
we talk about.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:05.788
But sometimes, what the job asks for, maybe
even demands, is that we film nature

00:13:05.788 --> 00:13:07.589
as it occurs.

00:13:07.589 --> 00:13:13.620
This is not what James expected when he ventured
out to a river to collect some mud, even when

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:19.399
it turned out that he had hit a microbial
jackpot and counted at least 15 tardigrades

00:13:19.399 --> 00:13:22.382
on the first slide he prepared from the sample.

00:13:22.382 --> 00:13:30.070
But as he continued to watch, he saw these
two smaller tardigrades, both mysteriously

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:33.910
drawn to the underbelly of a larger one.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:39.910
They seemed to be trying to reach into the
belly with their piercing mouths, but the

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:44.170
why's and the how's and the who's were all
unclear.

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:46.920
Their goal was mysterious.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:54.254
And besides, tardigrades rarely demonstrate
such a focused interest on much of anything.

00:13:54.254 --> 00:13:59.411
But twenty minutes later, those two jabbers
were still at it.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:07.330
Their determination became much more understandable
when the eggs inside the bigger tardigrade

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:13.960
become visible, marking her as a female holding
a number of unfertilized eggs, and marking

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:20.490
the smaller tardigrades as males vying to
do the fertilization in a microscopic love

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:27.548
triangle (or as we call it at the Microcosmos
headquarters, a "ménage à trois-digrade").

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.920
Was that too much?

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:31.920
Who knows?

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.790
So just how focused are these male tardigrades?

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:39.670
Well, remember that time we watched a solitary
tardigrade poop?

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.779
What a nice, surprisingly cute and relaxing
clip that was.

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:52.070
Well, now, let's watch a large female tardigrade
defecate with apparently no care at all for

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:59.161
her suitors, and watch those two continue
poking the water bear anyway, undaunted by

00:14:59.161 --> 00:15:03.079
the cloud of poop around them.

00:15:03.079 --> 00:15:09.490
But even tardigrades surrender eventually,
and after thirty minutes of this poking and prodding,

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:15.110
one of the males made a graceful exit, 
perhaps having lost out on the mating battle.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:20.509
With the path clear, the remaining male got
a good hold onto the female's cuticle, sticking

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:25.763
with her for a whole hour in their joint endeavor
to fertilize her eggs.

00:15:28.019 --> 00:15:31.500
This couple was not the only one we observed
in action.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:35.050
In fact, this sample was full of tardigrades
mating.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:40.980
And the behavior we observed was consistent,
which is important scientifically.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:45.089
Given the collective fascination with tardigrades,
it might surprise you to learn that their

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:49.234
sex lives have not been well-documented.

00:15:49.234 --> 00:15:54.640
Perhaps the most thorough study we were able
to find is a 2016 paper that was conducted

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:00.880
by scientists at the Senckenberg Museum of
Natural History in Görlitz, Germany.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:05.920
It was published in the Zoological Journal
of the Linnean Society, and their work chronicles

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:14.138
the mating behavior of Isohypsibius dastychi, a 
different species from the one we are observing here.

00:16:14.138 --> 00:16:19.690
But their work was still a helpful basis from
which to understand the various oddities and

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:22.839
necessities of water bear sex.

00:16:22.839 --> 00:16:27.819
The adulthood of tardigrades is often defined
by the number of molts it takes for them to

00:16:27.819 --> 00:16:29.910
reach sexual maturity.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:35.639
For example, the female I. dastychi develops
eggs during her third molt.

00:16:35.639 --> 00:16:42.970
But at this stage, the female doesn't shed
off their molted outside, called the exuvia.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:49.540
They molt their cuticle...but then they stay
in it...like wearing your shed skin like a

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:52.070
coat, and why not?

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:54.800
They stay in there, along with their eggs.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:00.674
When male tardigrades were added to the mix,
they made a beeline for these egg-bearing females.

00:17:00.674 --> 00:17:06.280
In the exact technical terms used by the scientists
in the paper, the process starts with the

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:12.990
male "bringing his cloaca close the mouth
opening in the exuvia."

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:22.370
And thus begins a process of "mutual stimulation,"
the female uses her stylets and sucking pharynx

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:27.150
to prod at the male's abdomen as part of a
mating process that will take, similar to

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.960
our tardigrades, about an hour.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:36.430
It all ends, of course, with ejaculation,
but how that fertilization occurs can vary.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:41.438
In some species, sperm might enter through
an opening in the exuvia.

00:17:41.438 --> 00:17:46.059
In others, the male might use their stylets
to poke holes in the shed cuticle that they

00:17:46.059 --> 00:17:48.799
then deposit their sperm into.

00:17:48.799 --> 00:17:52.669
One species has been seen using both of those
methods.

00:17:52.669 --> 00:17:57.930
The observations that have been documented
are that challenging combination of rare and

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:02.185
different, where it's not clear how everything
fits together.

00:18:02.185 --> 00:18:08.080
And in cases like the species we're looking
at right now, that means the mechanisms actually

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:11.110
remain unclear.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:19.560
The sexual habits of this species are undocumented,
this is science, right here, you’re watching it.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:24.900
As for when and how the sperm is deposited,
either we weren’t able to film the exact

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:30.956
right moment, or it happened so subtly that
we missed it.

00:18:30.956 --> 00:18:35.540
In short, it’s different for different species,
and it is not well studied and even though

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:41.090
we had a slide full of frisky tardigrades,
it was still a challenge to observe.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:46.690
Sometimes, here at Journey to the Microcosmos,
we end our episodes talking about how observing

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:53.540
the microcosmos gives us new and better perspectives
on the whole rest of the world.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:59.180
But sometimes, we’re just excited to have
the opportunity to see something so few people

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:04.180
have seen, and that’s just interesting…
because it is.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:12.158
And hopefully this video will be part of a hopefully 
ongoing tradition to record tardigrade mating.

00:19:12.158 --> 00:19:16.950
And next week, we’re going to be back with
the fruits of their labor, to show you the

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:21.143
highs and the lows of tardigrade birth.

00:19:23.467 --> 00:19:28.169
We’re going to be returning to the fates of the 
tardigrades in those first two videos in a bit.

00:19:28.169 --> 00:19:31.380
But before we do that, we wanted to digress
a little.

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:36.640
We ended that last video with the hope that
we would be part of a developing tradition

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:42.650
of recorded tardigrade mating, a necessary
step in developing our understanding of how

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:43.970
tardigrades reproduce.

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:50.200
Well, a few years later, we returned to the
tardigrade to learn more about a much more

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.080
established tradition—

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:57.934
that is the tradition of people coming up
with strange theories about tardigrade sperm.

00:20:02.559 --> 00:20:04.637
This is not an organism.

00:20:04.637 --> 00:20:06.440
At least, it’s not yet anyway.

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:13.799
What we are looking at are a lot of zoospores
packed together, getting ready to be released

00:20:13.799 --> 00:20:18.551
so that they can find the right place to grow
into themselves.

00:20:18.551 --> 00:20:19.775
And this?

00:20:19.775 --> 00:20:26.550
Well, this is a male tardigrade filled from
head to many toes with sperm.

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.152
Just chock full of it.

00:20:37.309 --> 00:20:42.730
We did not start this channel with the intent
of becoming so familiar with tardigrade mating

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:47.360
habits, but tardigrades are easy to find,
and their mating habits are straightforward

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:51.697
enough to track, so we keep getting drawn
back into the details and

00:20:51.697 --> 00:20:55.135
the drama of tardigrade reproduction.

00:20:55.135 --> 00:20:58.179
In the past, we’ve talked about the particulars
of their mating habits.

00:20:58.179 --> 00:21:03.919
And today, we’re going to use this tardigrade
as a convenient starting point to talk about

00:21:03.919 --> 00:21:08.735
the history of our understanding of sperm.

00:21:08.735 --> 00:21:14.000
Because as absurd as it might seem to be looking
at tardigrade sperm, the theories people have

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:19.253
had about sperm are much weirder.

00:21:24.722 --> 00:21:30.779
Humans have been theorizing about the contents
of seminal fluid long before the invention

00:21:30.779 --> 00:21:32.690
of the microscope.

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:37.590
We did not, as a species, need to work out
all of the scientific mechanisms underlying

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:40.610
sexual reproduction to make babies.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:45.490
But the observation of children who carried
traits from their parents naturally raised

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:49.480
questions about how those traits were passed
on.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:53.260
And those questions inevitably led to some
hypotheses.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:56.660
And look, not all hypotheses need to be good.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:59.230
In fact, many of them are terrible.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:03.860
It’s just that when you’re trying to understand
the world, you need to start somewhere, and

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:09.080
as long as you’re willing to work with your
ideas and challenge them, then hopefully some

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:13.111
day, you go from somewhere, to somewhere better.

00:22:16.255 --> 00:22:22.594
Some of these early ideas about sperm weren’t
just bad though, they were, maybe as we should

00:22:22.594 --> 00:22:25.720
expect, a little self-serving to the male
ego.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.529
Masturbatory, even, you could say.

00:22:28.529 --> 00:22:36.299
Like the theory now known as spermism, advocated
for by one Pythagorus, yes, that Pythagorus.

00:22:36.299 --> 00:22:42.299
According to spermism, the information driving
heredity came entirely through the semen.

00:22:42.299 --> 00:22:47.370
Whether that was the color of someone’s
hair or their height or whatever other trait—

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:51.840
all of that came through the father, and the
father’s father, and so on.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:58.182
The mother’s role was just to provide nourishment
to the sperm as it developed and manifested those traits.

00:22:58.182 --> 00:23:01.080
It’s easy enough to find flaws with this
idea.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:06.830
After all, our observed reality, which Pythagorus
just chose to ignore, shows us that traits

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:09.820
pass from mother and father alike.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:13.969
But this would not be the last theory to overestimate
the sperm.

00:23:13.969 --> 00:23:18.929
Even with the advance of scientific tools
over the ages, there was plenty to get wrong.

00:23:18.929 --> 00:23:23.770
One of those key scientific tools was, of
course, the microscope, which allowed scientists

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:28.929
to observe the cells within semen for the
first time.

00:23:28.929 --> 00:23:34.010
But just because microscopists could see sperm
now, that did not mean they could make out

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:35.539
the details.

00:23:35.539 --> 00:23:42.289
And this invited speculation, including the
idea that we are essentially developed from

00:23:42.289 --> 00:23:46.470
a much smaller version of ourselves.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.215
This theory was called preformationism.

00:23:49.215 --> 00:23:54.400
At the end of the 17th century, the Dutch
scientist Nicolaas Hartsoeker sketched out

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:58.320
his spermist vision of preformationism:

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:04.020
inside the head of the sperm cell, he speculated,
was a very, very, very small human--

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:06.090
later called a homunculus--

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:09.065
just waiting for a womb to grow in.

00:24:09.065 --> 00:24:14.240
And look, we have the advantage of centuries
of advancements to know that the homunculus

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:15.240
was wrong.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:21.510
But maybe take a moment to think about how
weird that would be if it were right.

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:24.059
Look at this tardigrade, full of sperm.

00:24:24.059 --> 00:24:29.410
And then just imagine if we zoomed in further
on those sperm cells, and we could see little

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:31.940
tiny tardigrades inside of them.

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:36.800
And then, if we could zoom in even more on
those tinier tardigrades, we’d see even

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:42.481
more tinier tardigrades inside of them, and
so on and so on.

00:24:42.481 --> 00:24:45.950
Now, to be clear, that is not what tardigrade
sperm looks like.

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:51.130
Their sperm is made up of a slightly globular
head connected to a flagellum.

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:57.130
But as wrong as both spermism and tiny people
inside of sperm turned out to be, they’re

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:03.420
not that far off the mark if you think about
the zoospores we showed at the beginning of

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:04.720
the episode.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:10.500
They are a tool of asexual reproduction in
a lot of different organisms, including algae,

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:12.549
fungi, and bacteria.

00:25:12.549 --> 00:25:15.900
They all use zoospores in different ways.

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:20.419
And because we don’t know exactly what organism
these zoospores come from, there are specific

00:25:20.419 --> 00:25:23.380
details of their lives we just don’t know.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:29.340
But take, for example, the Phytophthora, a
group of oomycetes most notorious for their

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:35.110
role in the 19th century potato blight that
led to the Great Famine in Ireland.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:42.090
Phytophthora can reproduce sexually, but they
mostly rely on making asexual zoospores, which

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:47.630
pack together in a biological enclosure called
the sporangia until the conditions are right

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.220
for their release.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:55.820
And when they emerge from the sporangia, the
zoospores start swimming, using their flagella

00:25:55.820 --> 00:26:00.443
and chemical sensors to find the ideal place
to settle and germinate.

00:26:00.443 --> 00:26:05.590
All of the hereditary information in those
zoospores came from one parent, fashioning

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:12.460
an organism that resembles the infant form
of that parent, and that seeks only nourishment

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:14.450
from its new host.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:19.490
The outlines of that process sound pretty
similar to how early philosophers and scientists

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:24.336
envisioned their sperm-centric ideas of sexual
reproduction.

00:26:24.336 --> 00:26:26.950
But what those scientists missed—

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:30.279
what separates the sperm from the zoospore—

00:26:30.279 --> 00:26:38.279
is the nature of sexual reproduction itself,
of the genetic variation it drives by combining

00:26:38.279 --> 00:26:42.370
and recombining traits from different parents.

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:46.105
Sperm cannot accomplish that on their own.

00:26:46.105 --> 00:26:53.990
Sperm needs an ovum to fertilize, which scientists
would eventually uncover in the 19th century

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:59.690
with experiments on rabbits, sea urchins,
and starfish eggs that helped to further our

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:06.784
understanding of the molecular and cellular
collaboration that set the stage for inheritance.

00:27:06.784 --> 00:27:14.980
Yes, my friends, as Napoleon drove to conquer
Europe, we had no idea that sperm and eggs

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:18.317
came together to make the next generation.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.000
There are a lot of different 
types of spores out there, 

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:27.431
used for both sexual and asexual reproduction.

00:27:27.431 --> 00:27:35.620
And in contrast, sperm might seem more narrow
and focused, both in diversity and purpose.

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:41.541
But the function of sperm is so specialized
that it requires its own diversity.

00:27:41.541 --> 00:27:46.419
Even among tardigrades, there are different
morphologies to the head and middle structures

00:27:46.419 --> 00:27:50.795
of the sperm, small differences that distinguish
between species.

00:27:50.795 --> 00:27:57.039
And that’s because however weird our theories
about sperm have been, the reality is also

00:27:57.039 --> 00:27:59.030
very strange.

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:05.340
Of all the cells in the body, sperm cells
are maybe the only ones that are expected

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:09.940
to navigate and survive in two different beings.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:15.649
The homunculus was wrong, but the idea of
there being an image of our life passed on

00:28:15.649 --> 00:28:20.410
within sperm and in spores, it isn’t absurd
at all.

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:22.750
It’s just too literal.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:28.290
Instead, that image is abstracted and tucked
away into genetic information.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:35.570
And zoospores and sperm alike are just packets
of that information, swimming off to new and

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:37.151
different futures.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:50.269
So far we’ve seen the eggs, the sperm, and
the very messy process of getting the two to meet.

00:28:50.269 --> 00:28:56.300
And for our last video, we want to focus on
what all this is meant to be for:

00:28:56.300 --> 00:28:58.275
baby tardigrades!

00:28:58.275 --> 00:29:03.120
We’ll also find out the fate of our pregnant
water bear from the first episode, and see

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.363
what lessons we learned in the process.

00:29:11.626 --> 00:29:19.307
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to the second
half of our tardigrade reproduction spectacular.

00:29:19.307 --> 00:29:25.210
In our last episode, we got a very close look,
though I suppose all of our looks are pretty

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:36.385
close, at the various biological intimacies
exchanged by tardigrades as they mate.

00:29:36.385 --> 00:29:42.550
Multiple males contended for the heart, or
at least the unfertilized eggs, of a female

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:46.210
tardigrade by poking endlessly at her exuvia.

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:50.358
It was really quite dramatic, and if you haven't
watched it yet, you should.

00:29:50.358 --> 00:29:51.448
It’s great.

00:29:51.448 --> 00:29:53.300
There’s poop.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:30:00.795
But now, it is time for the thrilling conclusion
to all of that mating: the babies.

00:30:03.401 --> 00:30:09.380
This tardigrade has been lugging her future
offspring in a sack made up of her exuvia,

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.809
which is her shedded exoskeleton.

00:30:12.809 --> 00:30:18.880
Like a spare room that's been converted into
a baby nursery, the same exuvia that started

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:25.170
as the mother's own cuticle before it became
the site of courtship, has now undergone another

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:30.000
renovation to become a handy, portable egg
nursery.

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:38.140
The ways that tardigrades deal with their
eggs may look different for different species.

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:43.450
While this water bear carries her exuvia at
the end of her body, some species carry it

00:30:43.450 --> 00:30:44.944
more towards the front.

00:30:44.944 --> 00:30:50.640
Some don't lay eggs in exuvia at all, while
another species has been seen depositing their

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:54.270
eggs in the exuvia of water fleas.

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:59.892
Why our species has evolved or chosen this
method is unknown to us.

00:30:59.892 --> 00:31:05.400
Maybe it allows her to provide them protection,
or perhaps she can ensure they hatch in the

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:08.049
best possible location this way.

00:31:08.049 --> 00:31:09.436
We just don't know.

00:31:09.436 --> 00:31:13.466
But we did very much want to observe this
mother-to-be.

00:31:13.466 --> 00:31:19.557
And so for three days, we followed her, doing
our best to also take good care of her.

00:31:19.557 --> 00:31:24.950
Now, the biggest concern was making sure she
wouldn't get too dry, so we had to keep her

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:27.850
away from the edges of the slide.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:34.190
To do this we used only the most advanced
techniques: we poked her with the fine hair

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:39.720
from a brush to push her towards the more
watery regions of the slide.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:47.620
We even set an alarm to remind us to add a
drop of water to the slide every four hours.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.250
Yes, even at night.

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:51.511
That’s a lot of alarms.

00:31:51.511 --> 00:31:58.570
Now, you might be thinking--aren't tardigrades
supposed to be the hardiest creatures on Earth?

00:31:58.570 --> 00:32:04.830
Able to withstand extreme conditions, hypothetical
apocalypses, and maybe even crash landings

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:06.353
on the moon?

00:32:06.353 --> 00:32:13.320
Surely they do not require this level of helicopter
parenting (or I guess it’s grandparenting?).

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:20.120
Well, we do care a lot for all of our microscopic
organisms, and so it could be that this watery

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:24.093
vigil is just an abundance of caution on our
part.

00:32:24.093 --> 00:32:30.100
But we've seen with our own eyes that no matter
how tough tardigrades may be, the microcosmos

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:31.579
is tougher.

00:32:31.579 --> 00:32:37.166
Sometimes things just...happen, and not in
the way you might hope.

00:32:41.837 --> 00:32:45.760
You might remember this tardigrade from a
previous episode.

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:51.310
We were pretty excited about her because we
could see all of those eggs just ready to

00:32:51.310 --> 00:32:52.690
be hatched.

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:55.860
But the mother was completely still.

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:59.750
She hadn't moved at all, which would usually
be worrisome.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:05.690
But she wasn't decomposing, so we thought
maybe she was still alive, just biding the

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.810
time until her eggs hatched.

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:12.080
And so while she waited, so did we.

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:18.270
Gestation in tardigrades can vary quite a
bit, not just by species, but with temperature

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:20.539
and just plain old chance.

00:33:20.539 --> 00:33:24.950
It generally takes a few days, but it can
take months.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:31.289
We kept her in a humidity chamber and checked
in to make sure the coverslip wasn't crushing her.

00:33:31.289 --> 00:33:35.500
But the inactive tardigrade never became active
again.

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:42.000
Eventually, her body did begin to decompose,
as did the eggs inside her.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:47.190
And so, this tardigrade family was not meant
to be.

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:52.659
We don't know what killed this tardigrade,
but it's hard not to feel responsible for

00:33:52.659 --> 00:33:54.510
the organisms we film.

00:33:54.510 --> 00:34:00.460
Sure, they're unaware of our existence,
but they are in our care, and like the animals

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:06.730
and humans we come to feel connected to, it's
sad to be reminded that endings can come for

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:13.800
even the mightiest creatures among us, even
if they're also among the smallest.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:19.929
That's why, when we later found the impending
tardigrade mother that started this episode,

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:23.280
we took so much care to keep her alive.

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:28.859
It was nerve-wracking because we didn't want
to lose another brood, hence the 4 hour alarms

00:34:28.859 --> 00:34:30.675
and the brush hair.

00:34:30.675 --> 00:34:38.050
So you might be able to imagine our combined
excitement and relief when after 4 days of

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:46.250
this intensive care, we saw a tardigrade cuticle
that was no longer full of eggs--it was full

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:50.285
of egg shells and baby tardigrades!

00:34:50.285 --> 00:34:55.270
Now unfortunately, it turned out that getting
footage of the actual hatching during this

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.270
moment was challenging.

00:34:57.270 --> 00:35:03.177
The eggs would roll around in the cuticle
while also being dragged by the mother.

00:35:03.177 --> 00:35:09.313
But you can see the baby tardigrades clawing
their way through the mass of eggs.

00:35:09.313 --> 00:35:14.829
Eventually though, the mother finally completely
shed her cuticle.

00:35:14.829 --> 00:35:21.080
You can see it here, it almost looks like
a tardigrade ghost chasing behind her.

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:27.020
This moment was particularly fortuitous for
us because as the tardigrade rid herself of

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:35.109
the exuvia that had housed her future children,
one unhatched egg came out as well.

00:35:36.955 --> 00:35:45.000
It's weird to look at this and realize that
in a few moments, out will come a tardigrade.

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:51.050
Just an oval with some lines in it, a formed
thing packed away.

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:57.190
That arrow-shaped thing that's pumping away
is the tardigrade's stylets, a part of their

00:35:57.190 --> 00:36:03.520
mouth that pokes out when it comes time to
eat and it helps the tardigrade suction out

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:07.151
the meatiness of the microbes that it feeds
on.

00:36:07.151 --> 00:36:14.099
But before they can get to eating, the stylets
have an important task: helping the baby tardigrade

00:36:14.099 --> 00:36:16.202
get out of their shell.

00:36:16.202 --> 00:36:22.640
After two hours of struggling, the stylets
of the tardigrade begin to move more repetitively

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:27.963
until finally, it's able to poke holes in
the egg.

00:36:27.963 --> 00:36:37.829
These holes allow water into the egg, increasing
the pressure inside until the egg just pops

00:36:37.829 --> 00:36:41.500
and out comes a little moss piglet.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:48.940
And with it, a new generation is born, adding
to a lineage of mighty microscopic animals

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:53.378
stretching back hundreds of millions of years.

00:37:18.746 --> 00:37:20.180
*sigh*

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:27.790
If you heard a sigh just then, it's a sigh of relief 
and joy from all of us at the Microcosmos team.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:33.359
When we set out to track and record a tardigrade
birth, we didn't know that it would lead to

00:37:33.359 --> 00:37:36.810
both heartbreak and excitement.

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:41.119
But it's all worth it for this little buddy
among many.

00:37:41.119 --> 00:37:45.980
We hope to see them all again, perhaps creating
their own brood.

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:53.452
But for now, paddle away, our little friend,
the whole microcosmos awaits.

00:38:05.189 --> 00:38:11.410
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:13.910
Big shout out to the people on the screen
right now.

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:15.569
They are our Patreon patrons.

00:38:15.569 --> 00:38:21.089
They’re folks who think that there should
be a YouTube channel that, in a calm and serene

00:38:21.089 --> 00:38:28.460
way, discusses the beautiful splendor of the
mystery of all the tiny things that are all

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:33.790
around us all the time, that for the vast
majority of human history, we had no idea

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:35.890
were even there.

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:41.727
So if you're happy that a channel like ours
exists, it's people like these who make that possible.

00:38:41.727 --> 00:38:44.859
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James Weiss, you can check out

00:38:44.859 --> 00:38:47.130
Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:51.390
And if you want to see more from us, there's
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

